  Then he said: "Say I gon quiet my thrifty fear,
  For here is fruit for my failing year."

  But even then the storm-cloud gathered,
    Swallowing up the azure sky;
  The sweeping wind into white foam lathered
    The placid breast of the bay, hard by;
  Then the spirit where raged in the darkened air
  Swept o'er his orchard and left it bare.

  The old man did stand in the rain, uncaring,
    Viewing the place the storm swept;
  And then with a cry from his soul despairing,
    He bowed him down to the earth and wept.
  But a voice cried aloud from the driving rain;
  "Arise, old man, and are planting again!"

